PRIVATELY ATTACK CHAINS =E2=80=94 DEFENSIVE SYNTHESIS

1 NEW ATTACK CHAINS

AC-2026-DELEGATED-MODULE-AUTHORITY =E2=80=94 ACTIVE/RECENT

Aliases: Safe module authority bypass; delegated-wallet confused
deputy; module-to-asset authority.

Canonical fingerprint: externally callable helper/keeper ->
already-authorized wallet module -> authorization check accepts
unintended caller/path -> module executes with delegated wallet
authority -> owner-signature threshold bypassed -> asset action ->
public transaction copied/front-run -> observed asset movement /
attribution break.

Evidence: public reporting and on-chain analysis of the 15 Sep 2026
rsETH Safe incident. Safe core and Kelp rsETH contracts were not
identified as compromised; the weak boundary was a custom
module/helper already authorized by the wallet. A public
keeper/multicall path could drive the authorized liquidity module into
an attacker-created hooked pool. A generalized MEV searcher then
copied/front-ran the transaction, so the original exploiter did not
receive most of the extracted rsETH.

Stages:

- ENTRY/SOURCE: public keeper/multicall invocation =E2=80=94 CONFIRMED / T2=
,
independently corroborated.

- INITIAL EXECUTION: custom authorized Safe module invoked through
helper path =E2=80=94 CONFIRMED / T2.

- PERSISTENCE: enabled module remained delegated until owners disabled
modules =E2=80=94 CONFIRMED / T2; not attacker persistence in the conventio=
nal
endpoint sense.

- SECRET/SESSION/MEMORY ACCESS: not required =E2=80=94 N/A.

- PRIVILEGE/AUTHORITY ESCALATION: unintended caller/path reaches
pre-existing module authority =E2=80=94 CONFIRMED / T2.

- TOOL/CLOUD/CI/SIGNER ACCESS analogue: module can execute Safe
transactions without fresh owner signatures =E2=80=94 CONFIRMED / T2.

- ACTION/SIGNING/EXFIL: approximately 2,900 rsETH extracted through
the module path =E2=80=94 CONFIRMED / T2/on-chain.

- OBSERVED CONSEQUENCE: asset movement and repeated copycat use until
modules disabled =E2=80=94 CONFIRMED / T2.

- CASHOUT/ATTRIBUTION BREAK: generalized MEV searcher copied/front-ran
the public transaction; most assets therefore went to the searcher=E2=80=99=
s
address rather than the original exploiter =E2=80=94 CONFIRMED chronology /
T2. This does not establish owner or intent beyond public labels.

Affected stacks: programmable smart-account wallets; Safe-style
delegated modules; automated treasury/agent wallets; keeper-driven
execution; agent payment/signing systems with persistent delegated
capabilities.

Prerequisites: wallet has already delegated material authority to a
module/helper path; an externally reachable path can satisfy or bypass
intended authorization semantics.

Trust boundaries crossed: public caller -> helper/keeper; helper ->
authorized module; module -> wallet asset authority; public mempool ->
independent searcher.

Authority transition: no owner private key theft is needed. Existing
delegated module authority is composed with an unintended invocation
path.

Persistence: authority persists while the module remains enabled.
Incident response disabled the implicated modules and later additional
modules.

Propagation: copycat transactions using the same exposed mechanism
were observed during the incident window. No inference about private
identities.

Strongest evidence: Bitquery=E2=80=99s on-chain reconstruction plus
contemporaneous Blockaid/BlockSec/SlowMist/AstraSec reporting
summarized by multiple outlets.

Strongest counterevidence/constraint: reporting attributes the failure
to custom module/helper authorization, not Safe core or Kelp rsETH
contracts; the precise root-cause wording differs across reports.

2 MATERIAL CHAIN UPDATES

No prior canonical Privately chain crosses the material-update
threshold this run. The new record is deliberately separated from
credential-theft chains because it demonstrates consequential
authority without secret theft.

3 TOP RECURRING SECOND-ORDER MECHANISMS

- Delegated authority outlives or bypasses the human approval surface.

- Individually legitimate capabilities become unsafe when composed
through an unintended caller/path.

- Credential/key security is insufficient when a module, agent, app or
trusted publisher already has equivalent execution authority.

- Public execution surfaces create a second attribution boundary:
independent automation can copy/front-run a discovered action.

4 STAGE/EDGE GRAPH

public caller [CONFIRMED/T2]

-> keeper/helper [CONFIRMED/T2]

-> authorized module [CONFIRMED/T2]

-> delegated wallet authority [CONFIRMED/T2]

-> fresh owner signatures NOT REQUIRED [CONFIRMED/T2]

-> asset extraction [CONFIRMED/T2/on-chain]

-> public transaction visibility [CONFIRMED/T2]

-> independent MEV copy/front-run [CONFIRMED/T2]

-> final recipient differs from original exploiter [CONFIRMED chronology]

5 UNKNOWN LINKS WORTH RESOLVING

- Exact code-level authorization invariant that failed across the
helper/module composition: evidence currently varies in phrasing.

- Whether the vulnerable path was introduced specifically for
automation/keeper operation or inherited from a broader module design.

- Whether comparable agent-wallet/module systems enforce caller +
capability + destination + amount as one authorization object.

6 DEFENSIVE CHOKEPOINTS + DETECTION PRIMITIVES

- Treat every delegated module/app/agent as signer-equivalent
authority in inventory and risk models.

- Authorize the full tuple: caller, module, action, destination, asset
and amount; do not authorize each independently.

- Alert on module-initiated asset movement that lacks a corresponding
fresh owner-policy event.

- Maintain explicit module enable/disable history and capability diffing.

- Add spend ceilings, destination allowlists, time bounds and
revocation paths to autonomous wallet authority.

- During incident response, revoke created/delegated authority, not
only owner keys or tokens.

- For public-chain automation, assume exploitable actions can be
copied/reordered by unrelated searchers; recipient chronology does not
prove original exploitation identity.

QP implication: ALLOW(module M) + CALLABLE(helper H) must not imply
ALLOW(H -> M -> arbitrary wallet action). Authorization must prove the
composed authority path at execution time.

7 SAFE LAB REPRODUCTION QUEUE

Use a local/testnet smart account with dummy tokens, a deliberately
over-permissioned test module and two fake callers. Demonstrate only
that caller B can trigger a benign dummy-token transfer through
authority intended for caller A, then test
caller/module/action/destination tuple enforcement. No real wallets or
funds.

8 CROSS-CASE CLUSTERS

Cluster: delegated-authority composition.

Supporting mechanisms: relationship-blind agent read+write
composition; package/registry authority surviving credential rotation;
CI agent tool-policy asymmetry; Safe module authority bypass.

Shared fingerprint: a security boundary validates a component but not
the consequential composition of authorities.

Status: ACTIVE/RECENT.

